{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // GPIO4 (BOOTSEL button, see chipGpios below) is a genuine bidirectional
  // GPIO merely wired in parallel with the BOOTSEL bootloader-select
  // circuit — not a dedicated QSPI-flash-CS BOOTSEL silicon trick like the
  // plain Raspberry Pi Pico — so it is handled entirely via the chipGpios
  // "button" tag (full capability set retained), not via inputOnlyGpios.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (RP2040/RP2350 family button/LED/NeoPixel audit,
  // 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: Adafruit "Feather RP2040" (product #4884) — the base/original
  // Adafruit Feather RP2040, no radio, no special add-ons. Has a red
  // status LED (D13/GPIO13), an onboard single status NeoPixel with NO
  // separate power-gate pin (GPIO16, powered directly from 3.3V), and a
  // BOOTSEL button that — on Revision D and later boards only — is also
  // wired to GPIO4 for use as a normal digitalRead()-able input.
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  // 1. learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-feather-rp2040-pico/pinouts (official
  //    Adafruit guide) — quotes verbatim:
  //      "Above the USB C connector is the D13 LED. This little red LED
  //      is controllable in CircuitPython code using board.LED... Also,
  //      this LED will pulse when the board is in bootloader mode."
  //      "Above the pin labels for A0 and A1 is the status NeoPixel LED.
  //      In CircuitPython, the NeoPixel is board.NEOPIXEL."
  //      "The NeoPixel is powered by the 3.3V power supply..." (no
  //      separate power-gate/enable pin mentioned anywhere on this page,
  //      unlike the ItsyBitsy RP2040's GPIO16 NEOPIXEL_POWER).
  //      "On Revision D and later of the Feather RP2040, the BOOTSEL
  //      button is connected to GPIO4 for use in your code." — the
  //      revision letter is silkscreened in a circle on the underside of
  //      the board; earlier revisions (A-C) do NOT have this GPIO
  //      connection, GPIO4 is simply unused/floating on those boards.
  // 2. raw.githubusercontent.com/adafruit/circuitpython (main)
  //    ports/raspberrypi/boards/adafruit_feather_rp2040/pins.c — confirms
  //    D13/LED->GPIO13, NEOPIXEL->GPIO16, and (current, single,
  //    unversioned board definition — i.e. reflecting the currently
  //    shipping/supported hardware) BUTTON/BOOT->GPIO4. No
  //    NEOPIXEL_POWER alias exists in this file at all, corroborating
  //    source 1's "no separate power-gate pin" finding.
  // 3. raw.githubusercontent.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr (main)
  //    boards/adafruit/feather_rp2040/adafruit_feather_rp2040.dts —
  //    official upstream Zephyr board port, cross-referencing the same
  //    hardware. Quotes:
  //      led0: led_0 { gpios = <&gpio0 13 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>; label = "LED"; }
  //      ws2812: ws2812 { gpios = <&gpio0 16 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
  //        chain-length = <1>; color-mapping = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN
  //        LED_COLOR_ID_RED LED_COLOR_ID_BLUE>; }
  //    This independently CONFIRMS: D13 LED is active-high; onboard
  //    status NeoPixel data line is GPIO16, active-high WS2812 protocol.
  //    No gpio_keys/button node is present in this devicetree file — this
  //    is consistent with Zephyr's board port having been authored
  //    against (or conservatively targeting) pre-Revision-D hardware,
  //    where GPIO4 is not wired to anything; it does not contradict
  //    source 1/2's Revision-D+ finding.
  //
  // GPIO13 (PIN_LED, aliased as D13): CONFIRMED onboard red status LED,
  // active-high (source 1 + 3). digital_input dropped to match this
  // repo's established led_high precedent for dedicated onboard LED pins
  // (rpipico, adafruit_feather_scorpio, adafruit_itsybitsy); pwm_output
  // kept since arduino-pico's analogWrite() works on any RP2040 GPIO,
  // enabling brightness dimming. Checked against this board's own primary
  // bus aliases (MOSI=19, MISO=20, SCK=18, SS=17, SDA(wire0)=2, SCL
  // (wire0)=3, SDA(wire1)=24, SCL(wire1)=25) — no collision, no
  // pins_arduino.h edit needed.
  //
  // GPIO16 (PIN_NEOPIXEL, no D-alias — not present in this board's D0-D29
  // array, only reachable via the raw PIN_NEOPIXEL macro): CONFIRMED
  // onboard single status NeoPixel (WS2812) data line, output-only
  // (source 1 + 2 + 3), with NO separate power-gate pin (unlike the
  // sibling adafruit_itsybitsy board's GPIO16 NEOPIXEL_POWER — on THIS
  // board GPIO16 IS the NeoPixel data line itself, powered directly from
  // 3.3V with no gating GPIO at all). Tagged
  // ["digital_output","led_neopixel"], matching the adafruit_feather_
  // scorpio/adafruit_itsybitsy NeoPixel precedent (no digital_input/
  // pwm_output — NeoPixel protocol timing is driven via the RP2040 PIO
  // peripheral, not analogWrite/digitalRead). No bus-alias collision
  // (checked against the same primary aliases as GPIO13 above).
  //
  // GPIO4 (BOOTSEL button, no alias at all in this board's
  // pins_arduino.h): CONFIRMED wired in parallel with the BOOTSEL
  // bootloader-select circuit, usable as a normal digitalRead()-able
  // input when application code is running — but ONLY on Revision D and
  // later boards (source 1, explicit vendor caveat). Earlier revisions
  // (A-C) leave GPIO4 unconnected. This audit tags GPIO4 anyway because
  // Adafruit's own CircuitPython board definition for this product is a
  // single, unversioned file (source 2) that unconditionally maps
  // BUTTON/BOOT to GPIO4 — reflecting the currently shipping/supported
  // hardware Adafruit itself builds firmware for today; anyone still
  // running a pre-Revision-D unit simply has an inert extra GPIO where
  // the "button" capability quietly does nothing (safe failure mode, no
  // functional harm). Added as a brand-new chipGpios entry keyed by bare
  // GPIO number, the same mechanism used for adafruit_itsybitsy's GPIO13
  // BOOT button (which likewise has no pins_arduino.h alias). Raw pin
  // number 4 is directly usable via pinMode(4,...)/digitalRead(4) on
  // arduino-pico (RP2040 Arduino pin numbers equal raw GPIO numbers).
  // Polarity: active-low with the RP2040's internal pull-up enabled —
  // inferred from the standard RP2040-family BOOTSEL/BOOT-button wiring
  // convention (switch to GND, no external pull-up) that both sibling
  // boards' Zephyr devicetree ports document explicitly for their own
  // BOOT buttons (adafruit_feather_scorpio GPIO7, adafruit_itsybitsy
  // GPIO13, both GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW | GPIO_PULL_UP) and that a community
  // forum example corroborates (digitalio.Pull.UP used when reading this
  // pin in CircuitPython) — NOT independently confirmed by an official
  // Adafruit polarity statement for this specific board/pin, since
  // Zephyr has no board port targeting this GPIO to cross-check against
  // (see source 3 caveat above). Tagged
  // ["digital_input","digital_output","pwm_output","button"] — full
  // capability set retained (ordinary GPIO wired to a switch to GND, not
  // a dedicated/capacitive-only pin), matching the adafruit_feather_
  // scorpio GPIO7 / adafruit_itsybitsy GPIO13 BOOT-button precedent. No
  // bus-alias collision (checked against the same primary aliases as
  // GPIO13/GPIO16 above).
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/adafruit_feather/notes.txt
  "chipGpios": {
    "4":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output", "button"], "note": "BOOTSEL button — on Revision D and later boards only, wired in parallel with the BOOTSEL bootloader-select circuit for use as a normal input; unconnected on earlier revisions (safe no-op there). No alias exists in this board's pins_arduino.h. Confirmed via Adafruit's own learn.adafruit.com pinouts guide (\"On Revision D and later of the Feather RP2040, the BOOTSEL button is connected to GPIO4 for use in your code\") and CircuitPython's current pins.c (BUTTON/BOOT -> GPIO4). Active-low with internal pull-up assumed by convention (matches adafruit_feather_scorpio GPIO7 / adafruit_itsybitsy GPIO13 BOOT buttons), not independently Zephyr-confirmed for this exact pin." },
    "13": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED / D13 — onboard status LED, active-high. Confirmed via Zephyr's adafruit_feather_rp2040.dts leds node (gpios = <&gpio0 13 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>) and Adafruit's own pinouts guide (\"the D13 LED... controllable in CircuitPython code using board.LED... will pulse when the board is in bootloader mode\")." },
    "16": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel"], "note": "PIN_NEOPIXEL — onboard single status NeoPixel (WS2812), output-only data line, no separate power-gate pin (powered directly from 3.3V). Confirmed via Adafruit's own pinouts guide (\"the status NeoPixel LED... In CircuitPython, the NeoPixel is board.NEOPIXEL\"; \"The NeoPixel is powered by the 3.3V power supply\"), CircuitPython's pins.c (NEOPIXEL -> pin_GPIO16, no NEOPIXEL_POWER alias), and Zephyr's adafruit_feather_rp2040.dts onboard ws2812 node (gpio0 pin 16, active-high)." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other RP2040 board. This board's pins_arduino.h declares A0-A3 =
  // GPIO26-29 (NUM_ANALOG_INPUTS = 4), matching the chip-level default —
  // this audit found no reason to override it.
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
